# 3. Upgrade material balance to genuine hybrid balance

The current moiety theorem covers continuous flow only. If harvest, transfer, reclassification, disaster, trade, or institutional events produce state jumps, the balance requires jump terms.

Suppose

\[
r(t_k^+)=\Delta_r(r(t_k^-),h^-,a_k,\xi_k).
\]

Then

\[
\mathsf L^\top r(t)-\mathsf L^\top r(0)
=
\int_0^t\mathsf L^\top b(s)\,ds
+
\sum_{t_k\le t}
\mathsf L^\top
\bigl(r(t_k^+)-r(t_k^-)\bigr).
\]

### Proposed theorem

```latex
\begin{theorem}[Conditional hybrid moiety balance]
Let $r$ be absolutely continuous between a locally finite sequence
of event times and admit left and right limits at every event.
If $\mathsf L^\top\mathsf S=0$, then
\[
\mathsf L^\top r(t)-\mathsf L^\top r(0)
=
\int_0^t\mathsf L^\top b(X_s,u(s),\omega(s),s)\,ds
+
\sum_{t_k\le t}
\mathsf L^\top\!\left[r(t_k^+)-r(t_k^-)\right].
\]
An event is internally moiety-conserving precisely when its
corresponding summand vanishes. Nonzero summands must be classified
as declared boundary exchanges, measurement redefinitions, or
model inconsistency.
\end{theorem}
```

This turns the balance law into an audit tool for hybrid models rather than merely a continuous-time identity.

## Important wording change

Replace “\(r\) contains all mass-bearing compartments” by:

> \(r\) contains all represented mass-bearing compartments inside the declared accounting boundary, with all omitted transfers represented as boundary flows or acknowledged structural discrepancy.

Literal completeness is rarely empirically demonstrable. **Boundary-relative completeness** is scientifically defensible.

