# 4. Strengthen positivity to include event resets and impulses

Flow positivity alone does not guarantee positivity of a hybrid model. Add:

\[
\Delta_r\bigl(\mathbb R_+^{n_r},\ldots\bigr)
\subseteq
\mathbb R_+^{n_r}.
\]

Also require event times to be locally finite, or otherwise specify a solution concept for accumulations.

```latex
\begin{theorem}[Hybrid history-cone invariance]
Assume:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the RFDE flow is well posed between locally finite event times;
\item the quasipositivity condition holds on the boundary of the
nonnegative history cone;
\item every admissible reset map sends nonnegative pre-event states
to nonnegative post-event states;
\item impulses and boundary withdrawals are donor-limited.
\end{enumerate}
Then every nonnegative initial history generates a nonnegative
hybrid trajectory on its interval of existence.
\end{theorem}
```

A useful further distinction is:

\[
\text{positivity}
\;\neq\;
\text{boundedness}
\;\neq\;
\text{persistence}
\;\neq\;
\text{viability above thresholds}.
\]

A model can preserve \(r_j\ge0\) while driving \(r_j(t)\to0\), violating ecological or service constraints.

