# 5. Introduce a “quantifier ledger”

Many sustainability claims fail because the uncertainty order is left implicit. Every theorem should state one of the following:

\[
\exists \pi\ \forall w,
\qquad
\forall w\ \exists \pi_w,
\qquad
\exists \pi\ \mathbb P^\pi(\mathrm{safe})\ge1-\alpha,
\]

which are not interchangeable.

Add a short formal convention:

```latex
\paragraph{Quantifier convention.}
Unless otherwise stated, robust safety uses the lower-game order
\[
\exists\text{ non-anticipative strategy }\Pi
\quad\forall\text{ admissible disturbances, parameters,
observation errors, and implementation outcomes}.
\]
A statement of the form $\forall w\,\exists u_w$ is not treated as
an implementable robust-control result unless the current value of
$w$ is observed before the action is selected.
```

The ledger should separately record:

1. initial-state uncertainty;
2. parameter uncertainty;
3. exogenous disturbances;
4. observation noise;
5. model discrepancy;
6. implementation/compliance uncertainty;
7. stochastic versus adversarial semantics.

