# 6. Treat information as a partially ordered resource

Define \(\mathcal I_1\succeq\mathcal I_2\) if the coarser observation history can be causally generated from the finer one. Then finer information cannot reduce viability if it may be ignored.

```latex
\begin{theorem}[Information-refinement monotonicity]
If information structure $\mathcal I_1$ causally refines
$\mathcal I_2$, while the physical dynamics, authority,
implementation correspondence, and safety constraints remain fixed,
then
\[
\operatorname{IViab}_{\mathcal I_2}
\subseteq
\operatorname{IViab}_{\mathcal I_1}.
\]
\end{theorem}
```

This provides a rigorous comparative-statics result for monitoring investments.

However, better monitoring can appear harmful if it also triggers:

- mandatory interventions;
- sanctions or legal constraints;
- strategic disturbance responses;
- public disclosure effects;
- changes in institutional authority.

Those effects should be modelled as changes in \(\Gamma\), \(\mathcal E\), or the game dynamics—not attributed to information alone.

## Add a quantitative value of information

For safety margin \(q(X)=\min_i g_i(X)\), define

\[
V_T^{\mathcal I}(B)
=
\sup_{\Pi\in\Pi_{\mathcal I}}
\inf_{\text{compatible uncertainties}}
\min_{0\le t\le T}q(X(t)).
\]

Then

\[
V_T^{\mathcal I}(B)\ge0
\]

is equivalent to finite-horizon epistemic viability, and

\[
\operatorname{VoI}_T
=
V_T^{\mathcal I_1}(B)
-
V_T^{\mathcal I_2}(B)
\]

measures monitoring value in physical safety-margin units rather than entropy units.

