# 7. Add a theorem identifying the governability gap

For a compatible-state set \(B\), define the common robust safe-action set

\[
\mathcal U_{\mathrm{com}}(B)
=
\bigcap_{(\phi,\vartheta)\in B}
\left\{
u\in\mathcal U(\phi):
F(\phi,\vartheta,u,w)
\in T_{\mathcal C}(\phi)
\quad\forall w
\right\}.
\]

Then distinguish:

- **physical infeasibility:** some compatible state has no safe action;
- **epistemic infeasibility:** all states have safe actions, but their intersection is empty;
- **authority infeasibility:** a common safe action exists but cannot be prescribed;
- **implementation infeasibility:** a safe prescription may realize unsafe controls.

```latex
\begin{proposition}[Common-action obstruction]
Suppose no informative observation arrives before the next action
must be selected. If
\[
\mathcal U_{\rm com}(B)=\varnothing,
\]
then $B$ is not robustly viable under output feedback, even if every
physical state in $B$ is individually viable under full information.
\end{proposition}
```

This is a profound structural result because it identifies incomplete information as a loss of **common-action compatibility**, not merely inaccurate estimation.

