# 9. Add resilience as informational recovery, not merely physical return

A physical state can re-enter a full-information viability kernel while the institution remains unable to determine that it has done so. Recovery should therefore target a viable information state.

Let \(\mathcal E\supseteq\mathcal C_X\) be an emergency envelope. Define the informational capture basin as the set of initial beliefs from which a strategy guarantees:

\[
X(t)\in\mathcal E
\quad\text{until some }T,
\]

and

\[
(B_T,h_T)\in\mathfrak K_\infty.
\]

Define worst-case recovery time

\[
\tau_{\mathcal E}(B_0,h_0)
=
\inf_\Pi
\sup_{\text{uncertainties}}
\inf\{T:(B_T,h_T)\in\mathfrak K_\infty\}.
\]

This unifies:

- resilience;
- emergency governance;
- detection delay;
- restoration;
- adaptive capacity;
- institutional learning.

It also distinguishes safety from recoverability:

\[
\text{currently nonviable}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{irrecoverable},
\]

and

\[
\text{physically recoverable}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{institutionally recoverable}.
\]

---

# 10. Add safe learning and endogenous sensing

Actions often affect both the ecosystem and future information. Therefore belief updates must depend on controls:

\[
B_{k+1}
=
\Psi(B_k,h_k,a_k,Y_{k+1}).
\]

This creates a dual-control problem. A conservative action may preserve the state but prevent learning; an experiment may reduce uncertainty but consume safety margin.

Define an action as safely informative if it is tube-safe and contracts a belief-size functional \(R\):

\[
\sup_{y^+}
R\!\left(\Psi(B,h,a,y^+)\right)
<
R(B).
\]

A new domain obligation should ask:

> Does there exist a sequence of actions that is simultaneously safe, institutionally implementable, and sufficiently informative to enter a smaller viable belief?

This connects adaptive management, monitoring design, precaution, and experimentation without assuming that learning is automatically safe.

---

# 11. Introduce compositional “assume–guarantee” sustainability certificates

Large systems cannot generally be verified monolithically. Partition the material-functional system into modules \(M_i\) with interface flows \(z_{ij}\).

Each module should provide a contract:

\[
A_i:\quad z_{ji}\in\mathcal Z_{ji}^{\mathrm{adm}},
\]

implies

\[
G_i:\quad
X_i(t)\in\mathcal C_i,\qquad
z_{ij}(t)\in\mathcal Z_{ij}^{\mathrm{guar}}.
\]

If all guarantees satisfy the assumptions of connected modules, then the product safe set is invariant.

```latex
\begin{theorem}[Conditional compositional safety]
Suppose each subsystem admits a robust invariant certificate under
its stated interface assumptions. If the interconnection satisfies
all interface assumptions, shared controls are jointly feasible,
material interface fluxes are consistently signed and routed, and
the hybrid event composition is nonblocking, then the product of
the subsystem certificates is a robust invariant set for the
interconnected system.
\end{theorem}
```

This is especially important for:

- watersheds and jurisdictions;
- supply chains;
- energy–water–food systems;
- trade leakage;
- upstream/downstream interactions;
- nested governance.

The theorem must require **shared-control compatibility**; separate subsystem controllers may otherwise demand contradictory actions.

