# 13. Add thermodynamic admissibility as an optional extension layer

The present material closure should remain, but a far-reaching extension can introduce:

- energy stocks \(e\);
- exergy or available-work stocks \(a\);
- entropy production \(\sigma\ge0\);
- heat and low-grade waste pathways;
- conversion-efficiency constraints.

A generic extension is

\[
\dot e
=
S_e\nu_e+b_e,
\]

with

\[
\dot S_{\mathrm{entropy}}
=
\Phi_{\mathrm{entropy}}+\sigma,
\qquad
\sigma\ge0.
\]

Materially feasible circular flows can then be ruled out if they require impossible energy quality or conversion efficiency.

The paper should explicitly state:

> Material closure prevents unaccounted mass creation or disappearance. It does not imply energetic feasibility, unlimited recyclability, or thermodynamic sustainability.

This avoids converting the stoichiometric framework into an implicit perpetual-recycling model.

