# 17. Add distributional and procedural feasibility

Institutional success should not be reduced to aggregate physical control. Introduce an allocation variable \(a^{\mathrm{dist}}\) and constraints such as

\[
c_i(X,u,a^{\mathrm{dist}})\le \bar c_i,
\qquad
i=1,\dots,N,
\]

or rights constraints

\[
R(X,u,a^{\mathrm{dist}})\in\mathcal R_{\mathrm{adm}}.
\]

The institutionally admissible correspondence becomes

\[
\Gamma(B,h)
=
\left\{
(a,u):
\begin{array}{l}
u\text{ is legally and technically authorized},\\
a\text{ satisfies declared allocation and procedural constraints}
\end{array}
\right\}.
\]

This makes precise the possibility that a physically viable policy is not institutionally or distributively implementable.

It should not be claimed that fairness follows from viability. Instead:

\[
\text{just sustainability}
=
\text{physical safety}
\cap
\text{service feasibility}
\cap
\text{distributional admissibility}.
\]

