# 21. Suggested upgraded central claim

The papers should not claim a universal sustainability theorem. They can make the following stronger but defensible claim:

> Sustainability governability can be represented as viability of a hybrid information state whose elements encode all physical histories and model parameters compatible with institutional observations. Material accounting and positivity constrain the admissible physical dynamics; robust viability identifies physically defensible actions; belief-space viability identifies actions that are safe despite incomplete information; and institutional correspondences determine whether those actions can be authorized and reliably implemented. The resulting nested kernels provide both constructive safety certificates and distinct impossibility diagnoses.

That is far-reaching because it unifies physical, epistemic, temporal, and institutional limits. It remains scientifically plausible because every conclusion is conditional on explicit dynamics, uncertainty sets, observation models, institutional correspondences, and solution concepts.
